# Outerview local mock interviewer

Outerview is a local, terminal-first mock coding interviewer. It uses a local
`llama-server` instance for conversation and screen understanding; no cloud
inference, browser automation, database, or interview history is used.

## Setup (Windows PowerShell)

```powershell
py -3.14 -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
# Install the correct CPU or CUDA PyTorch wheel first from pytorch.org.
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python -m pip install -e .
Copy-Item config.example.toml config.toml
```

Install `espeak-ng` and make its executable discoverable on `PATH` before
using Kokoro. Run a compatible `llama-server` on `127.0.0.1:8080`, then verify
the machine before an interview:

```powershell
mock-interviewer --doctor --config config.toml
mock-interviewer --benchmark --config config.toml --write-recommendation recommended.toml
```

If a native dependency has no Python 3.14 wheel, create the environment with
Python 3.12 instead. GPU STT additionally requires a compatible CUDA/cuDNN
runtime; CPU STT is the safe default.

Download model artifacts before an interview and keep them outside version
control: the faster-whisper model directory defaults to
`models/faster-whisper-small.en`, while the GGUF and vision projector paths are
configured under `[llm]`. Start `llama-server` yourself with those GGUF paths.
Kokoro is forced into Hugging Face offline mode at runtime, so prime its model
cache during setup rather than allowing a mid-interview download.

## Run

```powershell
# Develop the interviewer logic without microphone, speakers, or screen capture.
mock-interviewer --text-mode --config config.toml

# Capture the configured screen and editor crops to calibration/ and exit.
mock-interviewer --calibrate --config config.toml

# Run the live pipeline (headphones required for reliable barge-in).
mock-interviewer --config config.toml --save-report report.md
```

In text mode, type `start`, `repeat the question`, `end interview`, or `quit`.
The text-mode path deliberately uses the same Brain, state machine, scoring,
and report as the live runtime.
